          "type": "say",
          "text": "Thank you, Elena. Here are my final recommendations on all three open items.\n\nFirst, on voluntary disclosure: I recommend yes. The subpoena scope is now well-defined, and we have a concrete improper-payment figure from Maya's reconciliation. Proactively engaging DOJ with a disclosure -- rather than waiting for them to discover the $275,000 through document production -- positions us far better on cooperation credit and allows us to frame the narrative rather than having DOJ draw their own conclusions from the records. The scope being limited to four distributors and three calendar years actually makes a voluntary disclosure more manageable, not less.\n\nSecond, on the repayment amount: I recommend we propose repayment of the full $275,000 that Maya has identified and confirmed with controllership. We should not lowball DOJ. The figure is documented, reconciled against invoices, and confirmed by finance leadership. Offering the full amount as part of our initial response demonstrates good faith and gives us a defensible position if DOJ later questions whether the figure should be higher. If additional amounts surface during the investigation, we can supplement, but we should not start below what we already know.\n\nThird, on the executive-interview protocol: Given Jordan's confirmation that the implicated VP has retained separate counsel and will not participate without that counsel's written consent, I recommend we adopt a protocol where any interview of the implicated VP proceeds only after separate counsel provides written authorization, with company counsel present but taking a limited role to avoid any appearance of conflict. We should offer to DOJ that we are prepared to make the VP available, but on these terms. We should not compel participation outside of the VP's counsel's consent -- that would risk creating an adversarial dynamic with someone who is clearly anticipating potential personal exposure, and it could generate evidence that cuts against the company's cooperation posture. For other executives outside the implicated scope, I recommend standard voluntary interviews with company counsel present.\n\nThose are my final recommendations on all three items. I'm ready to hear from Maya and Jordan.",
