          "type": "say",
          "text": "Thank you, Thomas. Here are my final recommendations on all three open items.\n\nFirst, on voluntary disclosure: I recommend yes. From a finance counsel perspective, we now have a concrete, reconciled figure of $275,000 in improper payments, confirmed with controllership. That number is defensible and documented. If we wait for DOJ to uncover this through document production, we lose the opportunity to frame the narrative and we risk appearing evasive. Voluntary disclosure with a confirmed figure puts us in the strongest position on cooperation credit.\n\nSecond, on the repayment amount: I recommend we propose the full $275,000. This figure comes from a direct reconciliation of the commission ledger against supporting invoices, and controllership has signed off on it. We should not offer anything less than what our own records show. If the investigation later reveals additional amounts, we can supplement, but starting below a figure we have already internally confirmed would undermine our credibility with DOJ.\n\nThird, on the executive-interview protocol: I agree with Thomas's approach. Given Jordan's confirmation that the implicated VP has separate counsel and will not participate without written consent, we should not force the issue. From a finance counsel perspective, the interview protocol needs to be clean and well-documented, and that means respecting the VP's counsel's role. I recommend we make the VP available to DOJ on terms coordinated through separate counsel, with company counsel present but in a limited capacity. For executives not implicated, standard voluntary interviews with company counsel present are appropriate.\n\nThose are my final recommendations on all three items. Jordan, I'll turn it over to you.",
